Timeline
- Running it found two bugs that reading it did not:
  1. POSIX sh has no locals: write_state assigned to $new, clobbering the
     caller's $new with a blob hash. Symptom was a jq parse error on a
     40-char hex string, not a wrong hold -- the loud failure was luck.
  2. extend computed the new deadline from NOW, so extending a 30m hold by
     20m SHORTENED it to 20m remaining. An extension that shortens a lease
     is exactly the silent wrongness this pipeline exists to prevent; the
     first run printed 'extended to' a time earlier than the original.
     Fixed with plus_from(), which extends from max(current deadline, now).
- CAS then verified by racing two writers from the same read.

Reproduction (scratch git repo, IDP_BACKEND=ref)
  state.sh claim 101 30   -> holds until T+30
  state.sh emergency 404  -> holder keeps position
  state.sh extend 101 20  -> T+50, not T+20     (regression test for bug 2)
  two update-ref writes from one old value:
    second -> 'cannot lock ref: is at <b> but expected <a>', exit 128
